@page "/start"
@import Code from "../components/Code.xzor"
@import { href } from "../routes"

<section class="page narrow">
  <h1>Get started</h1>
  <p class="lead">One command sets up an empty folder or an existing project.</p>

  <div class="two">
    <Code name="install-win" />
    <Code name="install-unix" />
  </div>

  <p>The installer:</p>
  <ol class="checklist">
    <li>installs <a href="https://bun.sh" rel="external">Bun</a> if you don't have it;</li>
    <li>adds the compiler, the runtime and TypeScript 7;</li>
    <li>runs <code>xzor init</code>: it creates <code>package.json</code>, <code>tsconfig.json</code> and <code>.gitignore</code>, or adds only what's missing to the ones you already have, plus a starter app if there is no <code>main.ts</code>;</li>
    <li>installs the VS Code extension if <code>code</code> is on your PATH.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Run it</h2>
  <Code name="run" />

  <h2>Prefer to do it by hand?</h2>
  <Code name="manual" />
  <p>The settings <code>xzor init</code> makes sure of:</p>
  <Code name="tsconfig" />

  <h2>The editor</h2>
  <p>
    The extension gives you highlighting, completions, hover, go to definition and live errors, including inside the
    <code>@@</code> expressions and the <code>@@code</code> block. Install <code>xzor-&lt;version&gt;.vsix</code> from the
    <a href="https://github.com/pedro-gilmora/source-crafter-xzor/releases" rel="external">releases page</a>.
  </p>

  <a class="button primary" href="@href.Learn({ step: 1 })">Next: learn it in six steps</a>
</section>
